WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.790
The first 20 plus years of Long Now has
really been us kind of acting as a

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:13.290
start-up and executing on some of the
first projects, like the 10,000 year

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:19.170
Clock, which is nearing completion now in West Texas, and certain library projects

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:23.400
like the Rosetta project. Laura's here
today. She'll be talking a bit about

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:29.010
language and culture as well. And
that first 20 to 25 years, we're now

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:37.079
looking at as Long Now's Q1. And
if that's our Q1 project what is our Q2

00:00:37.079 --> 00:00:44.670
project? And when we started this,
we realized very much that engineering

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:49.739
a 10,000 year clock is a difficult problem, but engineering a

00:00:49.739 --> 00:00:53.579
ten thousand year institution is really
the hardest problem that we could ever

00:00:53.579 --> 00:00:57.899
work on. And so I think that this is
really going to be Long Now's next big

00:00:57.899 --> 00:01:02.370
project: how to build long term
institutions. And as I started diving

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:06.000
into it, I realized there's
really no discipline,

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.840
there's no broad study of this anywhere.
Nobody is a long-term

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:16.049
institution expert. And so this seems
like the thing for Long Now to do—not

00:01:16.049 --> 00:01:20.280
only for ourselves as the first customer
but also for the world.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:24.780
There has to be some great practices and
knowledge that we can pull together and

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:28.920
be the clearinghouse for this kind of
thing. So this is the very first

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:35.640
gathering in this space. And nobody here
is really from a very long-term

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:38.970
institution necessarily. I think that's
going to be our next one of these

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:41.909
gatherings, is going to be bringing those
people together. But this one is the one

00:01:41.909 --> 00:01:46.640
where we get to think very very broadly.
And we're looking at natural systems,

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:54.600
language and culture, some of that
historical precedence of the past and

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:58.770
how these things work. And I've pulled
together my research so far on long-term

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:02.610
institutions. And what I want today to be
as much as possible is a discussion

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:09.279
among all of us.

